1
00:00:07,059 --> 00:00:10,240
Welcome to the Golden Rule.

2
00:00:11,919 --> 00:00:13,160
Why did you say it like that, Vin?

3
00:00:13,820 --> 00:00:16,239
Well, actually, I don't know. I just wanted this to be very dramatic.

4
00:00:16,500 --> 00:00:20,719
Because, you know, in every industry, in every area of expertise, there's always these golden rules,

5
00:00:20,879 --> 00:00:24,480
and sometimes they're cheesy, sometimes they're good, sometimes they're not that good.

6
00:00:24,559 --> 00:00:27,140
But I promise you, this one is very good.

7
00:00:27,519 --> 00:00:29,379
The Golden Rule when it comes to communication.

8
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:34,560
This is a useful rule, especially when, you know, often I'll have my students ask me questions like,

9
00:00:34,560 --> 00:00:38,479
Oh, Vin, I want to know, is my accent a problem when I communicate?

10
00:00:38,899 --> 00:00:42,000
Vin, I use a lot of ums and ahs. Is that a problem, Vin?

11
00:00:42,320 --> 00:00:46,299
Vin, what about my movement? Am I moving around too much when I communicate?

12
00:00:46,600 --> 00:00:48,200
Am I moving my hands too much?

13
00:00:48,439 --> 00:00:55,060
So I get a bunch of questions like this, and you can answer all of them by applying the Golden Rule.

14
00:00:55,719 --> 00:01:02,219
And the Golden Rule simply is, if it distracts people from the message, it's a problem.

15
00:01:02,799 --> 00:01:03,759
Let me say that again.

16
00:01:03,979 --> 00:01:04,540
If it distracts people from the message, it's a problem.

17
00:01:04,560 --> 00:01:09,120
If it distracts people from the message, it's a problem.

18
00:01:09,480 --> 00:01:13,560
If you're moving your hands too much while you're communicating and it distracts people from the message,

19
00:01:13,680 --> 00:01:15,900
then yes, it is a problem.

20
00:01:16,480 --> 00:01:19,900
Now, another one, for example, let me throw you to a video,

21
00:01:20,019 --> 00:01:24,019
and let's see if you can identify what's distracting you from the message

22
00:01:24,019 --> 00:01:25,700
when you see one of my students communicate.

23
00:01:26,040 --> 00:01:27,239
So let's have a quick look.

24
00:01:28,459 --> 00:01:33,819
He gave us a good deal in regards to the minimum requirements.

25
00:01:33,819 --> 00:01:40,459
He gave us a good deal in regards to the minimum requirements to actually fulfill an order for these plastic bags.

26
00:01:40,739 --> 00:01:49,239
And we pursued the avenue of trying to find customers for these boutique bags.

27
00:01:50,219 --> 00:01:52,280
Why am I struggling? I'm not lifting anything.

28
00:01:52,500 --> 00:01:54,560
Everything you see here is done by video editing.

29
00:01:54,700 --> 00:01:57,200
It's done by the good old Craig Gibbons.

30
00:01:57,859 --> 00:02:00,959
But I just struggle anyway to make this video more engaging.

31
00:02:01,739 --> 00:02:06,219
With that example you just saw on video, what was distracting from the message?

32
00:02:07,040 --> 00:02:09,860
It was the ums and ahs. It was the filler words.

33
00:02:10,099 --> 00:02:16,000
It was the non-words, the sounds that he was making to distract you from the message.

34
00:02:16,219 --> 00:02:21,479
So what that means in that particular situation is that the non-words and the filler words were a problem.

35
00:02:21,759 --> 00:02:23,979
It was distracting people from the message.

36
00:02:24,199 --> 00:02:28,580
Therefore, you've got to get rid of the non-words and the filler words or decrease them dramatically.

37
00:02:28,919 --> 00:02:30,860
So can you see how the golden rule is applied?

38
00:02:30,960 --> 00:02:35,840
Anytime something is distracting people from the message, it's a problem.

39
00:02:36,360 --> 00:02:42,439
Now you know the epic golden rule in the world of communication.

40
00:02:42,759 --> 00:02:44,659
And I hope it helps you on your journey.

